@h1 Nodes & core vocabulary h1@

One mechanic underlies every structure in Wit. Once you have it, the whole
language opens up, because everything else — headings, lists, tables, figures,
your own reusable components — is built from this single move.

@h2 The one mechanic h2@

A @strong node strong@ wraps a span of content in a named structure. You write an
opening name, the body, and a matching closing name:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@

@blockquote A quotation, set apart. blockquote@
pre@@@

The opener is @@@code @name code@@@, the closer is @@@code name@ code@@@ — the same
word with the @code @ code@ on the other side — and the body is everything
between. That is the entire idea. @@@code @h1 … h1@ code@@@ becomes an
@@@code <h1> code@@@; @@@code @blockquote … blockquote@ code@@@ becomes a
@@@code <blockquote> code@@@. The renderer decides whether a node is a block or an
inline span, so you never have to.

Some nodes hold nothing — a horizontal rule, an image — and those close
themselves. You just write the opener:

@@@pre
Above the line.

@hr

@img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
pre@@@

@h2 Nesting, and why indentation is only for your eyes h2@

Nodes nest inside nodes. A list is a @@@code @ul code@@@ whose body is a run of
@@@code @li code@@@ items:

@@@pre
@ul
  @li First item li@
  @li Second item li@
  @li Third item li@
ul@
pre@@@

That renders to @@@code <ul><li>First item</li> … </ul> code@@@. Notice the
indentation. It is there @strong for you strong@, not for the parser — scope
comes entirely from the open and close pairs, never from columns. You could write
the whole list flush-left and it would mean exactly the same thing. In fact
@code wit fmt code@ will re-indent a file to match its nesting without changing a
single structural relationship. Think in pairs, not in whitespace.

@h2 A tour of the built-in vocabulary h2@

You don't define any of these. They ship with Wit, each mapping to one semantic
HTML shape. Reach for them the moment you want them.

@h3 Headings h3@

Six levels, @@@code @h1 code@@@ through @@@code @h6 code@@@, mapping to
@@@code <h1> code@@@ … @@@code <h6> code@@@:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@
@h2 Chapter One h2@
@h3 A Quiet Morning h3@
pre@@@

@h3 Inline marks h3@

The marks that live inside a sentence. Bold and italic have the shortcuts
@code *bold* code@ and @code _italic_ code@; the rest are nodes:
@@@code @code code@@@ for inline code, @@@code @u code@@@ and @@@code @s code@@@ for
underline and strikethrough, @@@code @sub code@@@ and @@@code @sup code@@@ for
subscript and superscript, @@@code @mark code@@@ to highlight,
@@@code @small code@@@ for fine print, and @@@code @br code@@@ for a line break.

@@@pre
Water is H @sub 2 sub@ O, noted on the 2 @sup nd sup@ of March.
The reading was @mark unusual mark@ but @small within tolerance small@.
pre@@@

@h3 Lists h3@

Bulleted @@@code @ul code@@@ and numbered @@@code @ol code@@@, both filled with
@@@code @li code@@@ items; plus definition lists — @@@code @dl code@@@ wrapping
@@@code @dt code@@@ terms and @@@code @dd code@@@ descriptions:

@@@pre
@ol
  @li First, light the lamp. li@
  @li Then, log the hour. li@
ol@

@dl
  @dt Keeper dt@
  @dd The one who tends the light. dd@
dl@
pre@@@

@h3 Links & media h3@

Links with @@@code @a code@@@, images with @@@code @img code@@@, and captioned
figures with @@@code @figure code@@@, @@@code @img code@@@, and
@@@code @figcaption code@@@ — plus @@@code @audio code@@@ and @@@code @video code@@@:

@@@pre
Visit @a |href https://example.org| Example a@.

@figure
  @img |src ./figure.png| |alt A figure|
  @figcaption A descriptive caption. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 The block @@@code @figcaption code@@@ currently renders
wrapped in a stray @@@code <p> code@@@ inside the figure. It's harmless and the
caption still shows correctly — just don't be surprised by the extra tag in the
output. aside@

@h3 Blocks h3@

The block-level shapes: @@@code @p code@@@ for an explicit paragraph,
@@@code @blockquote code@@@ for a quotation, @@@code @pre code@@@ for preformatted
text, and @@@code @hr code@@@ for a horizontal rule:

@@@pre
@blockquote
  A quoted passage worth remembering.
blockquote@

@pre
  function example() { return 1; }
pre@

@hr
pre@@@

@h3 Sectioning h3@

The document-outline elements: @@@code @section code@@@, @@@code @article code@@@,
@@@code @aside code@@@, @@@code @header code@@@, @@@code @footer code@@@,
@@@code @nav code@@@, and @@@code @main code@@@. They nest to describe a document's
shape:

@@@pre
@article
  @header
    @h1 Article Title h1@
  header@
  @section
    @p A first paragraph. p@
  section@
  @footer
    @small Published 2024. small@
  footer@
article@
pre@@@

@h3 Tables h3@

@@@code @table code@@@, with the parts @@@code @thead code@@@, @@@code @tbody code@@@,
@@@code @tr code@@@, @@@code @th code@@@, @@@code @td code@@@, and
@@@code @caption code@@@. You can write rows inline, or — the payoff — generate the
whole table from your data:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Site, Status], [Dunmore Head, operational]]| |caption Stations|
pre@@@

@h3 Layout h3@

@@@code @row code@@@ and @@@code @col code@@@ place content side by side (they
render as an invisible flexbox), and @@@code @div code@@@ and @@@code @span code@@@
are the generic block and inline containers when nothing more specific fits:

@@@pre
@row
  @col Left. col@
  @col Right. col@
row@
pre@@@

@h2 Parameters carry metadata, bodies carry content h2@

You've seen @@@code @a |href …| text a@ code@@@. The link's @strong address strong@
rode in a parameter; the link's @strong text strong@ was the body. That split is
the governing rule of the whole language: @strong content goes in the body,
metadata goes in parameters strong@. A parameter never carries the content of a
node.

There are a few ways to write parameters — pipes @@@code |k v| code@@@, parentheses
@@@code (k v) code@@@, and record braces @@@code { k: v } code@@@ — and choosing
between them is a topic of its own in the reference. For now, one rule covers it:
if it's the thing the reader reads, it's the body; if it merely describes the
node, it's a parameter.

@h2 You don't define these — they ship h2@

Everything on this page is built in. When you want a structure the core doesn't
provide — a styled callout, a chapter template, a citation format — you
@strong define your own strong@ node and use it exactly the same way. That's a
later step; the built-in vocabulary already covers most of what a document needs.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Putting content in a parameter. strong@ Parameters are metadata. The link text, the quote, the caption — those belong in the body. li@
@li @strong Expecting indentation to create scope. strong@ It doesn't; only the open and close pairs do. A misindented file is still correct — an unclosed node is not. li@
@li @strong A comment as the first line of a node body. strong@ A @code ~ code@ comment placed immediately below an opener, with no blank line after it, silently collapses the following blocks into one paragraph. Always leave a blank line after a comment inside a node. li@
@li @strong Forgetting the close. strong@ Every paired node needs its @@@code name@ code@@@. Leave it off and you get @code E_UNCLOSED_NODE code@ pointing at the opener. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

For the complete list of built-in nodes and the elements they produce, see the
@a |href /docs/reference/core-vocabulary/| core vocabulary reference a@. To see
where nodes sit among Wit's layers, read
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@. And to practise, work
through @a |href /docs/start/tutorial/| the tutorial a@, which builds a real
document one node at a time.
